   160	\begin{abstract}\noindent
   161	{\small We consider the problem of finding integer triangles with $R/r$ a positive rational, where $R$ and $r$ are the radii of the circumcircle and an excircle, respectively.
   162	We show that for general triangles $R/r>1/4$ applies.
   163	The equation $R/r=N$ turns out to be related to the elliptic curve $\mathcal{E}_N$ given by $v^2=u^3+2(2N^2+2N-1)u^2-(4N-1)u$.
   164	If $N>1/4$ is rational, then the torsion group of $\mathcal{E}_N$ is $\Z/2\Z\times\Z/6\Z$ if $N(N+2)$ is a square and $\Z/6\Z$ otherwise.
   165	We show that a rational triangle with rational ratio $R/r=N$ exists if and only if $N>1/4$ and there exists a rational non-torsion point on the curve $\mathcal{E}_N$ which satisfies a certain 
   166	condition. Furthermore, we show that the rank of $\mathcal{E}_N$ is positive when 
   167	$N = m^2 \pm 1>1/4$ for a rational $m$. We also show that on 
   168	every curve $\mathcal{E}_N$ whose rank is positive, there are  infinitely many 
   169	rational points which lead to infinitely many non-similar integer triangles with $R/r=N$.}
   170	\end{abstract}
   171	
   172	\section{Introduction}\label{sec-1}
   173	Consider a triangle with sides of integer length $f, g, h$. 
   174	Prominent circles associated with the triangle are the circumcircle
   175	which passes through the three vertices, the incircle, and the three excircles which have the three sides as tangents.
   176	% (and differ from the incircle).  %%% Everybody knows what in- and excircles are!
   177	The radii of the circumcircle $C$ and one of the three excircles $E$ are denoted in the following by $R$ and $r$, respectively (see Figure~\ref{fig1}).
   178	Let $d$ be the distance between the 
   179	centres of these two circles. 
   180	It is a standard result 
   181	in triangle geometry that 
   182	\begin{equation}\label{euler}
   183	d^2 = R(R+2r)
   184	\end{equation}
   185	(see, e.g.,~\cite[Theorem 295]{johnson}).
   186	% Proof see also https://www.cut-the-knot.org/triangle/EulerIO.shtml
   187	%
   188	%This relation should not be confounded with the Euler-Chapple formula for the circumcircle and the incircle. \dc{Give a citation to \cite{Chapple}, in its suitable place?}
   189	%Euler's theorem in geometry (first published by 
   190	%Chapple~\cite{Chapple}), it is a standard result 
   191	%in triangle geometry that 
