   239	\begin{lem}
   240		\label{lem1}
   241		$R/r>1/4$.
   242	\end{lem}
   243	\begin{proof}
   244	If $r$ is the radius of an excircle and $r > R$, then, since the vertices of the triangle lie outside the excircle, we have $d + R > r$, i.e., 
   245	$d > r - R$. By \eqref{euler}, we obtain
   246	$$d^2 = R^2 + 2Rr > R^2 - 2Rr + r^2,$$ 
   247	which implies $4R > r$, i.e., $R/r > 1/4$. 
   248	\end{proof}
   249	\begin{comment}
   250	\dc{What about assuming the condition $R>r$ in the above proof (to be consistent to Fig~1 and Fig~4 and the example in Section~7) and prove like this: $\ldots$}
   251	\cb{If $R>r$, then $R/r>1$ and $1>1/4$.}
   252	\end{comment}
   253	
   254	Equally well known are the formulas 
   255	\begin{equation}
   256		\label{R,r}
   257		R=\frac{fgh}{4\Delta}, \quad r=\sqrt{\frac{s(s-f)(s-g)}{s-h}}
   258	\end{equation}
   259	where $r$ is the radius of the excircle touching $h$ from outside, $s=(f+g+h)/2$ is the semi-perimeter, and $\Delta=\sqrt{s(s-f)(s-g)(s-h)}$ the area of the triangle. Hence, we have
   260	\begin{equation}
   261		\label{R/r}
   262		\frac{R}{r}=\frac{2fgh}{(f+g+h)(f-g-h)(g-h-f)}.
   263	\end{equation}
   264	Observe that for non-degenerate triangles, the triangle inequality applies, i.e., the denominator on the right hand side of~\eqref{R/r} is different from $0$. 
   265	\begin{comment}
   266	\dc{What about writing ``strictly positive" instead of ``different from 0"?}
   267	\cb{We just need different from $0$, otherwise, the reader may think that
   268	``strictly positive" is essential.}
   269	\end{comment}
   270	
   271	It should be noted that we  can always scale a triangle with rational sides to a similar triangle with integer sides without changing the value of $R/r$.
   272	It is interesting to speculate whether the ratio $R/r$ could ever be an integer $N$ for a  triangle with integer sides. In \cite{MacLeod}, MacLeod studied a similar problem for such  triangles with $R/\rho$ an integer, where $R$ and $\rho$ are the radii of the circumcircle and incircle, respectively. He showed that these  triangles are relatively rare, and by brute force found only a handful such integer triangles. It is interesting to see that our problem, although very similar to MacLeod's, 
   273	behaves quite differently. Indeed, we will see in Section~\ref{sec-2} that our problem leads to finding certain rational non-torsion points on the elliptic curve $\mathcal{E}_N$ given by $v^2=u^3+2(2N^2+2N-1)u^2-(4N-1)u$. In other words, the existence of integer triangles with rational ratio $R/r=N$ is equivalent to the existence of special non-torsion points on the curve $\mathcal{E}_N$. The torsion group of $\mathcal{E}_N$ and the torsion points which are needed later are determined in Section~\ref{sec-torsion}.
   274	Section~\ref{sec-4} identifies the non-torsion points that correspond to real triangles.
   275	So, a necessary condition for the existence of such triangles is that the rank of $\mathcal{E}_N$ is positive. We will show in Section~\ref{sec-3} that this is in particular the case for the values  $N=m^2\pm 1$ for any rational number $m\geq 2$.
   276	We then show in Section~\ref{sec:triangles} that whenever the rank of $\mathcal{E}_N$ is positive for a rational $N>1/4$, 
   277	  infinitely many pairwise non-similar integer triangles with $R/r=N$ exist. 
   278	We conclude in Section~\ref{sec:poncelet} with an application of our results to Poncelet's porism.
   279	%In the next section, we first show how we can associate a rational point on $\mathcal{E}_N$ (for $N>1/4$) to a rational triangle with given value $R/r=N$. 
