   296	If we multiply~\eqref{4} by the denominator and move all terms to the left, we can rewrite~\eqref{4} as a cubic equation in the variables $f,g,h$:
   297	\begin{equation*}
   298		%\label{5}
   299		Nf^3-N(g-h)f^2-(g^2N+2gh(N-1)+h^2N)f+N(g-h)(g+h)^2=0.
   300	\end{equation*}
   301	%We cannot get very far with this form, but we can proceed quickly 
   302	Now, we replace $h$ by $2s - f - g$ and obtain the following quadratic equation in $f$:
   303	\begin{equation}
   304		\label{6}
   305		gf^2+\big(g^2+2s(2N-1)g-4s^2N\big)f-4(g-s)s^2N=0.
   306	\end{equation}
   307	Since $f$ is rational, the quadratic equation~\eqref{6} must have a discriminant
   308	which is a rational square, so that there must be a rational $d$ with
   309	\begin{equation*}
   310		%\label{7}
   311		d^2=g^4+4s(2N-1)g^3+4s^2(4N^2-2N+1)g^2-32N^2s^3g+16N^2s^4.
   312	\end{equation*}
   313	If we substitute $d=s^2y$ and $g=sx$, we get the quartic equation
   314	\begin{equation}
   315		\label{8}
