  1123	\begin{lem}\label{lem:4}
  1124	If $\mathcal{E}_N$ has positive rank for a rational number $N>1/4$, then there are rational points $R=(u_R,v_R)$ on $\mathcal{E}_N$ that satisfy condition~\eqref{eq-13'} and $u_R>1$.
  1125	\end{lem}
  1126	\begin{proof}
  1127	According to Lemma~\ref{lem:1a}, there are rational non-torsion points that satisfy condition~\eqref{eq-13'}.
  1128	Now, suppose $1-4N<u_R<0$, then $R+T_6^--T_3^-$ satisfies condition~\eqref{eq-13'} and the $u$-coordinate of this point is larger than $1$.
  1129	\end{proof}
  1130	\begin{lem}\label{lll}
  1131	If $\mathcal{E}_N$ has positive rank for a rational number $N>1/4$, then there are infinitely  many rational points $R=(u_R,v_R)$ on $\mathcal{E}_N$ that satisfy $u_R>1$.
  1132	\end{lem}
  1133	\begin{proof}
  1134	According to Lemma~\ref{lem:4}, there are rational non-torsion points that satisfy condition~\eqref{eq-13'} and
  1135	whose $u$-coordinates are larger than $1$. If $R_0=(u_0,v_0)$ is such a point, then the point $-(2R_0+T_3^-)$ is also a
  1136	point that satisfy condition~\eqref{eq-13'} and whose $u$-coordinate is  larger than $1$. Iterating this process yields a sequence
  1137	$R_0, R_k=(-1)^k (2^k R_0+J_k T_3^-)$, where $(J_k)_k$ is the Jacobsthal  sequence modulo 3, i.e., $J_k\equiv -k \mod  3$. 
  1138	\begin{comment}
  1139	\dc{Why? Reference and a bit more explanation!} 
  1140	\end{comment}
  1141	Recall that 
  1142	$T_3^-$ is a torsion point of order~$3$. In particular, the sequence $R_k$ is not periodic, and infinitely many non-similar
  1143	triangles result from the sequence of points $R_k$.
  1144	\end{proof}
  1145	As   a result, we obtain the following.
  1146	\begin{cor}
  1147	If $\mathcal{E}_N$ has positive rank for a rational number $N>1/4$, then there are infinitely many rational and integer triangles with $R/r=N$.
  1148	\end{cor}
