   341		v&=-\frac{2N(x-2)(8N^2x+2Nx^2-8N^2+2Ny-x^2)}{x^3}.
   342	\end{align}
   343	%$$
   344	%and the inverse map by
   345	%$$
   346	%\begin{aligned}
   347	%x&=-\frac{4N(v+2Nu)}{(u-1)(u+4N-1)}, \\
   348	%y&=-\frac{4N(u^2+4N-1)(8N^2u+4Nu+4Nv+u^2-4N-2u+1)}{(u-1)^2(u+4N-1)^2}.	
   349	%\end{aligned}
   350	Thus, starting with the rational triangle with sides $f,g,h$ and rational ratio $R/r=N$, we can find a rational point $(u,v)$ on
   351	the cubic curve $\mathcal{E}_N$. Using the equations in the opposite direction, we can also reconstruct the sides $f,g,h$ from $(u,v)$, at least up to scaling.
   352	To do so, use~\eqref{eq-x} and~\eqref{eq-y} to find $(x,y)$ on $\CN$ and then the formulas in Theorem~\ref{th:1} to compute the sides of the triangle.
   353	However, not every rational point $(u,v)$ on $\mathcal{E}_N$ leads to numbers $f,g,h$ which are the sides of a triangle.
   354	So our next task is to identify those rational points $(u,v)$ on $\mathcal{E}_N$ that lead to {\em positive\/} values of $f,g,h$ which
   355	satisfy the {\em triangle inequalities} $f+g>h, g+h>f$ and $h+f>g$. The three triangle inequalities can be equivalently expressed
   356	by the single inequality
   357	$$
   358	 (f + g - h) (f - g + h)(-f + g + h) (f + g + h)>0
   359	$$
   360	or, also equivalently,
   361	$$
   362	(f^2+g^2+h^2)^2>2(f^4+g^4+h^4).
   363	$$
   364	Observe that if we replace $N$ by $-N$ in \eqref{9}, we get 
   365	MacLeod's curve~\cite[eq.~(9)]{MacLeod}. Thus, our discussion 
   366	also covers MacLeod's problem in the case when $N$ 
   367	is a rational number.
   368	%In some sense, our problem covers the related MacLeod's Problem for negative integers $N$. \textcolor{red}{... we would have to explain this.} \cb{In other words, by changing $N$'s in MacLeod's family $v^2=u^3+2(2N^2-2N-1)u^2+(4N+1)u$ to $-N$'s we get our family being studied in this work.}  
   369	
   370	Also notice that if $R/r$ is rational for a rational triangle for just one of its three excircles, then all ratios of radii of the three excircles, the incircle, and the circumcircle are rational.
   371	Another remark is that the formula~\eqref{4} is symmetric in $f$ and $g$. Hence, if we start with a rational point $(u,v)$ on $\mathcal{E}_N$ which
   372	corresponds to a triangle with sides $f,g,h$, then we can find another rational point $(u',v')$ on $\mathcal{E}_N$ which belongs to the mirrored triangle with sides $g,f,h$.
